1
00:00:03,935 --> 00:00:09,971
When a water main breaks, someone has to decide, quickly, which valves to close.

2
00:00:10,185 --> 00:00:16,662
It's harder than it sounds. Water networks are full of loops, and one stuck valve can make a shutdown much bigger.

3
00:00:16,888 --> 00:00:20,684
Closing the wrong area can also cut off homes that are nowhere near the break.

4
00:00:20,888 --> 00:00:25,717
ShutOff keeps the network as a graph in FalkorDB. This town is simulated.

5
00:00:25,935 --> 00:00:30,752
A caller reports water gushing on Maple Street, between Second and Third Avenue.

6
00:00:30,966 --> 00:00:37,815
ShutOff locates that pipe, then asks FalkorDB which pipes stay connected if every working valve acts as a wall.

7
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:43,620
That group is the smallest area that can be shut off. The four valves around it are the ones to close.

8
00:00:43,872 --> 00:00:48,260
Before anything changes, ShutOff tests the plan on a copy of the graph.

9
00:00:48,482 --> 00:00:54,843
The break is cut off from every source. 34 accounts lose water, and no critical customer is affected.

10
00:00:55,060 --> 00:01:01,712
Each claim comes with a path from the graph: the hospital keeps a route from the tank, 14 connections long.

11
00:01:01,919 --> 00:01:08,292
ShutOff also warns about a risk: valve V-265 has not been exercised since 2011.

12
00:01:08,513 --> 00:01:13,609
The crew suggests closing only the two valves nearest the break. ShutOff checks that first.

13
00:01:13,825 --> 00:01:20,268
It fails. The purple line is the path FalkorDB found: water still reaches the broken pipe from the reservoir.

14
00:01:20,482 --> 00:01:22,165
Two valves are not enough.

15
00:01:22,685 --> 00:01:27,676
What if valve V-265 cannot be closed? ShutOff has already worked that out.

16
00:01:27,903 --> 00:01:34,938
This preview is hypothetical: seven valves, a dialysis account cut off, and nothing recorded.

17
00:01:35,153 --> 00:01:41,654
Now the crew confirms that V-265 is seized. That is saved in the graph, and the plan is recalculated.

18
00:01:44,075 --> 00:01:50,193
Seven valves now. 66 accounts lose water, and one critical customer is cut off.

19
00:01:50,403 --> 00:01:57,287
It's a home-dialysis account on Willow Court: outside the shut-off area, but its only supply runs through it.

20
00:01:57,497 --> 00:02:03,731
ShutOff never closes anything by itself. The supervisor must name the critical customer before approving.

21
00:02:03,966 --> 00:02:08,075
The server then re-checks the plan, and refuses if anything has changed.

22
00:02:08,310 --> 00:02:14,451
Approval records work order WO-1001 and marks the valves closed in the graph.

23
00:02:14,653 --> 00:02:19,192
This is a simulation: no crew is dispatched, and nothing is sent to anyone.

24
00:02:19,716 --> 00:02:25,938
So, why use a graph database? Every decision here is a question about what is connected to what.

25
00:02:26,153 --> 00:02:35,080
In FalkorDB, that's a direct question: components find the area, a graph copy tests the plan, shortest paths show the proof.

26
00:02:35,294 --> 00:02:44,175
We also checked the segmentation against the U.S. EPA's WNTR toolkit on public networks. It matched exactly.

27
00:02:44,388 --> 00:02:50,018
ShutOff models connectivity, not water pressure, and it doesn't control real valves.

28
00:02:50,247 --> 00:02:54,577
Within those limits, every recommendation it makes is checked and explained.
